The dynamic relation between the plant inputs and each of the plant outputs $y_j(t),\, j=1,\hdots,n_y$, is given by:
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
y_j(t)&=\sum\limits_{i=1}^{n_u}\sum\limits_{k=1}^{m}h_{ji}(k)\zeta\left(\mathcal{L}_k(a,q),u_i(t)\right)+d_j(t)= H_j^T\varphi(t)+d_j(t),
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{L}_k(a,q),\, k=1,\hdots, m$ are the basis transfer functions defined by the parameter $a$ that are selected by the control designer, $q$ is the time shift operator ($qu(t)\doteq u(t+1)$) and the operator $\zeta\left(\mathcal{L}_k(a,q),u(t)\right)$ denotes the output of a linear system represented by the transfer function $\mathcal{L}_k(a,q)$ at the time step $t$, when the signal $u$ is applied as its input:
\begin{equation} \zeta\left(\mathcal{L}_k(a,q),u(t)\right)\doteq \sum\limits_{l=1}^{\infty}\Psi_k(a,l)u(t-l),
\end{equation}
where $\Psi_k(a,l)$ are the impulse response coefficients of the basis function $\mathcal{L}_k(a,q)$.

By defining the matrix $H\in {\mathbb R}^{n_y\times n_um}$ as $H \doteq \left[H_1, \hdots, H_{n_y}\right]^T$, the dependence of the plant output on the regressor and the disturbance vectors can be written as:
\begin{equation} y(t)=H\varphi(t)+d(t).
\end{equation}

Under Assumptions \ref{A:disturbance} and \ref{A:system_class}, the goal is to control the plant in order to track a desired output reference and reject disturbances from $t=0$ up to some finite time step $T$, where the time horizon $T$ can be very large ($T\gg m$). In addition, we assume that the control inputs $u(l), l=-T_1,\hdots,-1$ are known, where the horizon $T_1$ should be long enough such that the effects of $u(-T_1-1)$ on the regressor vector $\varphi(0)$ are small. The error in the regressor vector coming from the initial state can be accounted for by embedding it in the bound on $d(t)$.  Moreover, the controller shall enforce input and output constraints. Such a control objective can be formalized by the following optimization problem:
\begin{eqnarray} &
\begin{aligned}
\min\limits_{u(0),\hdots,u(T-1)}\sum_{t=0}^{T} &\left( y(t)-y_{\text{des}}(t) \right)^TQ \left( y(t)-y_{\text{des}}(t) \right)+u(t)^T S u(t) + \Delta u(t)^T R \Delta u(t) \end{aligned}\\ &\begin{aligned}
& \text{subject to},\,\forall t\in[0,T] \\
&\begin{array}{lllll}
Cu(t)&\leq &g\\
L \Delta u(t)&\leq&f\\
E y(t)&\leq& p
\end{array}
\end{aligned}
\end{eqnarray}
where $y_{\text{des}}(t)\in {\mathbb R}^{n_y}$ is the desired output reference, $Q \in {\mathbb R}^{n_y\times n_y}$, $S\in {\mathbb R}^{n_u\times n_u}$ and $R \in {\mathbb R}^{n_u \times n_u}$ are positive semi-definite weighting matrices selected by the control designer, and $\Delta u(t)=u(t)-u(t-1)$ is the rate of change of the control input. The element-wise inequalities in \eqref{Eq:IO_constraints} define convex sets through the matrices $C\in{\mathbb R}^{n_i\times n_u}$, $L\in{\mathbb R}^{n_{\Delta_u}\times n_u}$, $E\in{\mathbb R}^{n_o\times n_y}$ and the vectors $g\in{\mathbb R}^{n_i}$, $f\in{\mathbb R}^{n_{\Delta_u}}$, $p\in{\mathbb R}^{n_o}$, where $n_i$, $n_{\Delta_u}$ and $n_o$ are the number of linear constraints on the inputs, input rates and outputs, respectively.

Any stable transfer function can be represented by the following infinite sum:
\begin{equation}
G(q)=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}h(k)\mathcal{L}_k(a,q),
\end{equation}
where $q$ is the time shift operator ($qu(t)\doteq u(t+1)$) and $\mathcal{L}_k$ are mutually orthogonal transfer functions characterized by:
\begin{equation} \frac{1}{2\pi j}\oint \mathcal{L}_p(a,q)\mathcal{L}_k(a,q^{-1})\frac{dq}{q}=\delta_{pk},
\end{equation}
where $\delta_{pk}$ is the Kronecker delta function and the integral is around the unit circle. The parameter $a\in \mathbb{C},\, |a|\leq1$ defines the basis functions. The sequence of coefficients $h(k)$ is convergent as $\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}h(k)=0$.

For systems that exhibit oscillatory behavior, Kautz functions are more appropriate. These functions have the following form:
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
\mathcal{L}_{2k-1}(a,q)&=\frac{\sqrt{1\!-\!c^2}(q\!-\!b)}{q^2\!+\!b(c\!-\!1)q\!-\!c}\left[ \frac{-cq^2\!+\!b(c\!-\!1)q\!+\!1}{q^2\!+\!b(c\!-\!1)q\!-\!c}\right]^{k\!-\!1}\\
\mathcal{L}_{2k}(a,q)&=\frac{\sqrt{1\!-\!c^2}(1\!-\!b^2)}{q^2\!+\!b(c\!-\!1)q\!-\!c}\left[ \frac{-cq^2\!+\!b(c\!-\!1)q\!+\!1}{q^2\!+\!b(c\!-\!1)q\!-\!c}\right]_,^{k\!-\!1}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $a\in \mathbb{C}, \, |a|\leq1$ and $b=\frac{a+a^*}{1+aa^*}$, $c=-aa^*$ with $^*$ denoting the conjugate of a complex number.

The basis functions that give rise to an impulse response model are:
\begin{equation} \mathcal{L}_k(a,q)=aq^{-k}
\end{equation}

We assume that the gain, transport delay, poles and zeros are not exactly known, but they belong to the following set:
\begin{equation} \begin{array}{ccccc}
\underline{g}&\leq & g &\leq &\overline{g}\\
\underline{\tau} & \leq & \tau & \leq & \overline{\tau}\\
\underline{Z}&\leq & Z&\leq &\overline{Z}\\
\underline{P} &\leq & P &\leq &\overline{P}
\end{array},
\end{equation}
where the inequalities in \eqref{Eq:set_of_parameters} should be interpreted as element-wise inequalities and the bounds $\underline{g},\, \overline{g}, \, \underline{\tau}, \, \overline{\tau} \in {\mathbb R}$, $\underline{Z},\, \overline{Z}\in {\mathbb R}^{n_z}$ and $\underline{P},\, \overline{P}\in {\mathbb R}^{n_p}$ are assumed to be known.

Under these assumptions, the goal is, for a given values of parameter $a$ and the model order $m$, to find the upper and lower coefficient bounds $\overline{h}_{ji}(k)$ and $\underline{h}_{ji}(k), \, k=1,\hdots, m$ such that it holds:
\begin{equation} \underline{h}_{ji}(k) \leq h_{ji}(k) \leq \overline{h}_{ji}(k), \, k=1,\hdots, m.
\end{equation}

Then for any fixed $g$, $\tau$, $Z$ and $P$, the impulse response coefficients $\Psi (g,\tau, Z,P,l)$ can be represented as:
\begin{equation} \Psi(g,\!\tau,\!Z,\!P,\!l)=\sum\limits_{k=1}^{\infty}h(g,\!\tau,\!Z,\!P,\!k)\Psi_k(a,\,l), \!\forall l=1,\,\hdots,\, \infty,
\end{equation}
where, because of the orthogonality of the basis functions, the coefficients $h(g,\tau,Z,P,k)$ are given as normalized projections of $\Psi(g,\tau,Z,P,l)$ on the impulse responses of the basis functions:
\begin{equation} h(g,\tau,Z,P,k)=\frac{\sum\limits_{l=1}^{\infty}\Psi(g,\tau,Z,P,l)\Psi_k(a,l)}{\sum\limits_{l=1}^{\infty}\Psi_k(a,l)^2}.
\end{equation}

Therefore, the upper and the lower bounds \eqref{Eq:coeficient_bounds} can be calculated as:
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
\overline{h}_{ji}(k)&=\sup\limits_{g,\tau,Z,P} h(g,\tau,Z,P,k)\\
\underline{h}_{ji}(k)&=\inf \limits_{g,\tau,Z,P}h(g,\tau,Z,P,k)\\
&\text{subject to \eqref{Eq:set_of_parameters}},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

The contribution of the unmodeled dynamics on the transfer function from input $i$ to the output $j$ is then given by:
\begin{equation}
\eta_{ji}(t)=\sum\limits_{k=m+1}^{\infty}h_{ji}(k)\zeta \left(\mathcal{L}_k(a,q)u_i(t)\right).
\end{equation}

The contribution of the unmodeled dynamics is guaranteed to be bounded: $|\eta(t)|\leq \overline{\eta_{ji}}, \, \forall t$, where the bound $\overline{\eta_{ji}}$ is given by:
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
&\overline{\eta}_{ji}=\overline{u_i} \sup\limits_{g,\tau, Z,P}\sum\limits_{l=1}^{\infty}\left|\Psi(g,\!\tau,\! Z,\! P,\! l)-\sum\limits_{k=1}^mh(g,\!\tau,\! Z,\! P,\! k)\Psi_k(a,\! l)\right|\\
&\text{subject to \eqref{Eq:set_of_parameters}}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\overline{u}_i$ can be calculated as:
\begin{equation} \overline{u}_i=\max\limits_{Cu\leq g}|u_i|
\end{equation}

At a given time step $t$, we define the model set $\mathcal{F}(t)$ as the set containing all the matrices $H$ that are consistent with the Assumptions \ref{A:disturbance} and \ref{A:system_class} and the collected input-output data \eqref{Eq:measured_data}:
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
\mathcal{F}(t)\!\doteq\!
\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
H\!\in\! \mathcal{F}(0)\!:\!&-\epsilon_d\!-\!\epsilon_v\leq \tilde{y}(l)\!-\!H\varphi(l)\leq \epsilon_d\!+\!\epsilon_v,\,\forall l \in [0,t]
\end{array}\right\}\!\!.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

To this end, let us consider the following polytopes:
\begin{equation} \mathcal{F}_j(t)=\{H_j\in {\mathbb R}^{n_um}: A_j(t)H_j\leq b_j(t)\},\, j=1\hdots n_y.
\end{equation}

We note that for each $j$, the polytope $\mathcal{F}_{j}(t)$ can be calculated recursively in time as the intersection of the polytope $\mathcal{F}_{j}(t-1)$ and the two half spaces defined by the newly measured plant output, $\tilde{y}_j(t)$:
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
\mathcal{F}_{j}(t)=
&\mathcal{F}_{j}(t-1) \\
&\cap  \{H_{j}\!\!\in\!{\mathbb R}^{n_um}\!:\!\varphi(t)^TH_{j}\leq \tilde{y}_j(t)\!+\!\epsilon_{d_j}\!+\!\epsilon_{v_j}\}\\
&\cap  \{H_{j}\!\!\in\!{\mathbb R}^{n_um}\!:\! -\varphi(t)^TH_{j}\leq -\tilde{y}_j(t)\!+\!\epsilon_{d_j}\!+\!\epsilon_{v_j}\}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

Therefore, we introduce a regularization term, that penalizes the deviation of the new nominal model from the previous one, giving rise to the following LP:
\begin{equation} \begin{array}{c}
\max \limits_{\xi_j(t),H_{c,j}(t)} \sum \limits_{j=1}^{n_y}\xi_j(t)-\alpha\|H_{c,j}(t-1)-H_{c,j}(t)\|_1\\
\text{subject to}\\
a_{ji}(t)H_{c,j}(t)\!+\!\xi_j(t)\|a_{ji}(t)\|_2\!\leq\! b_{ji}(t), \, j=1,\hdots, n_y,\, i=1,\hdots, n_u,
\end{array}
\end{equation}
 where $\xi_j(t) \in {\mathbb R}$ is the radius of the maximum volume ball inscribed in $\mathcal{F}_{j}(t)$, $\alpha>0$ is a design variable, and $a_{ji}(t)$ and $b_{ji}(t)$ stand for the $i^{\text{th}}$ row of the matrix $A_j(t)$ and the vector $b_j(t)$.

Similarly, we define the vectors of future input increments $\Delta u(k|t), \, k\in [t,t+N-1]$ as:
\begin{equation}
\!\Delta u(k|t)\!=\!\begin{cases} u(t|t)\!-\!u(t-1)\!\! & \text{if}\, k=t\\
u(k|t)\!-\!u(k\!-\!1|t)\!\! & \text{if}\, t\!+\!1\leq k\leq t\!+\!N\!-\!1 \end{cases}
\end{equation}

Moreover, we define the future regressor vectors $\varphi(k|t)\in{\mathbb R}^{n_um},\,k\in[t+1,t+N]$ as:
\begin{equation} \varphi(k|t)\!=\!\!\begin{cases} W\varphi(t)\!\!+\!\!Zu(t|t)\!\!\! & \text{if} \,\, k=t+1\\
                                                 W \varphi(k\!-\!1|t)\!\!+\!\!Zu(k\!-\!1|t) \!\!\!& \text{if} \,\, t\!+\!2\!\leq\! k\!\leq\! t\!+\!N.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

In addition, we define the current prediction error $\hat{d}(t)\in {\mathbb R}^{n_y}$ as the difference between the measured plant output and the one predicted by the nominal model at the time step $t$:
\begin{equation} \hat{d}(t)\doteq \tilde{y}(t)-H_c(t)\varphi(t).
\end{equation}

Then, we consider the following cost function:
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
J(U,\tilde{y}(t),\varphi(t))\doteq \sum\limits_{k=t}^{t\!+\!N\!-\!1}\!&\!\left(\hat{y}(k\!+\!1|t)\!-\!y_{\text{des}}(k\!+\!1|t)\right)^TQ(\hat{y}(k\!+\!1|t)-\!y_{\text{des}}(k\!+\!1|t))\!+\! u(k|t)^TSu(k|t)\\&+\Delta u(k|t)^TR\Delta u(k|t),
\end{aligned}\!\!
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{equation} \hat{y}(k+1|t)=H_c(t)\varphi(k+1|t)+\hat{d}(t).
\end{equation}

For fixed values of $N$, $Q$, $S$ and $R$, we can now define the finite horizon optimal control problem (FHOCP) to be solved at time step $t$ (see step 3) of Algorithm \ref{Alg:RHCOP2}):
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
\min
\limits_{U} &J(U,\tilde{y}(t),\varphi (t))\\
&\text{subject to}\, \eqref{Eq:input_rate},\, \eqref{Eq:relaxed_constraints1}, \, \eqref{Eq:end_constraint},
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
which is a quadratic program (QP), that can be efficiently solved.

Then for the second stage, we compute the following quantities:
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
\overline{\epsilon}_j(k|t)=&\max \left\{ \overline{y}_j(k|t)-\hat{y}'_j(k|t),\, \hat{y}'_j(k|t)-\underline{y}_j(k|t) \right \}, k\in [t+1,t+N],\,\, j=1,\hdots, n_y,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\overline{\epsilon}(k|t)=[\overline{\epsilon}_1(k|t),\hdots, \overline{\epsilon}_{n_y}(k|t)]^T$, $\overline{\epsilon}_j(k|t)\geq 0$ denotes the maximal possible difference between the possible future output of the plant and the predicted output at time step $k$, and 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ccc}
\overline{y}_j(k|t)&=&\max\limits_{A_j(t)H_j\leq b_j(t)} H_j^T\varphi'(k|t)\\
 \underline{y}_j(k|t)&=& \min\limits_{A_j(t)H_j\leq b_j(t)} H_j^T\varphi'(k|t). \end{array}
\end{equation}

In addition, we define the matrix $\Phi(t+1|t)\in {\mathbb R}^{n_um\times n_um}$ that depends on the $n_um$ past regressor vectors and the first future regressor vector as:
\begin{equation}
\Phi(t+1|t)=\left[\begin{array}{cccc} \varphi(t-n_um+1) &\hdots & \varphi(t) & \varphi(t+1|t) \end{array} \right].
\end{equation}

Therefore, the optimization problem to be solved at the second stage of the control input calculation is given as:
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
&\max \limits_U \left|\det \Phi(t+1|t)   \right|\\
&\text {subject to}\\
&\eqref{Eq:input_rate}, \, \eqref{Eq:end_constraint},\\
&\left.\begin{array}{lll}
H\varphi(k|t)&\!\leq\!& \hat{y}'(k|t)\!+\!r\overline{\epsilon}(k|t)\\
H\varphi(k|t)&\!\geq\! &\hat{y}'(k|t)\!-\!r\overline{\epsilon}(k|t)\\
EH\varphi(k|t)\!+\!\overline{d}&\! \leq\!&\! p
\end{array} \right\}\begin{array}{ll}\forall H\in \mathcal{F}(t)\\ \forall k\in[t\!+\!1, t\!+\!N] \end{array}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $r\in {\mathbb R}$, $r\geq 1$ is a design parameter, selected by the control designer, that determines the allowed augmentation of the intervals containing the predicted outputs between the first and second stages. This parameter indicates by how much the bounds \eqref{Eq:bounds1} are allowed to be inflated.

However, in this specific case the infinite dimensional constraints can be reformulated into a set of linear constraints. In fact, if we define the matrix $E''$ and the vectors $\overline{d''}$ and $p''(k|t),\, k\in [t+1,t+N]$ as:
\begin{equation} E''=\left[ \begin{array}{c} I\\ -I\\ E \end{array} \right],\, \overline{d''}=\left[\begin{array}{c} \bold{0}\\ \bold{0}\\ \overline{d} \end{array}   \right],\, p''(k|t)=\left[ \begin{array}{c} \hat{y}'(k|t)\!+\!r\overline{\epsilon}(k|t)\\-\hat{y}'(k|t)\!+\!r\overline{\epsilon}(k|t)\\p\end{array} \right],
\end{equation}
where $I$ and $\bold{0}$ respectively denote the identity and zero matrices of apropriate size, then the infinite dimensional constraints in \eqref{Eq:FHOCP2_cost} can be rewritten as
\begin{equation}
E''H\varphi(k|t)\!+\!\overline{d''}\leq p''(k|t),\, \forall H\in \mathcal{F}(t),\, k\in[t\!+\!1,t\!+\!N],
\end{equation}
and these constraints can be written in the form of linear constraints by using the Lemma \ref{L:constraint_equivalence_1}.

Therefore, the optimization problem \eqref{Eq:FHOCP2_cost} can be solved by first solving the following two LPs:
\begin{eqnarray} J_1=\max \limits_{u(k|t)} k^T u(t|t)\\ J_2=\min \limits_{u(k|t)} k^T u(t|t) \end{eqnarray}
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\text{subject to}\\
\eqref{Eq:input_rate}, \, \eqref{Eq:end_constraint},\\
\left.\begin{array}{l}\!\!
A(t)^T\lambda_l(k|t)=\left[\begin{array}{c}
e_{l1}''\varphi(k|t)\\
\vdots\\
e_{ln_y}''\varphi(k|t)
\end{array}\right]\\
b(t)^T\lambda_l(k|t)\leq p_l''(k|t)\!-\!\sum\limits_{j=1}^{n_y}e_{lj}''\epsilon_{d_j}\\
\lambda_l(k|t)\geq \bold 0
\end{array}\!\!\! \right\}\begin{array}{cc}\forall l=1,\!\hdots,\!n_o\!+\!2n_y\\
\forall k\!\in\![t\!+\!1,t\!+\!N]\end{array}
\end{array}
\end{equation}
where $e_{lj}''$ stand for the element of the $l^\text{th}$ row and $j^\text{th}$ column of the matrix $E''$ and $p_l''(k|t)$ is the $l^\text{th}$ element of the vector $p''(k|t)$. The matrix $A(t)$ and the vector $b(t)$ are defined as in \eqref{Eq:explanation}. The solution to \eqref{Eq:FHOCP2_cost} can then be obtained by comparing the cost function values of the two LPs: if $|J_1+n|\leq |J_2+n|$, then the solution of the LP with the cost function \eqref{Eq:cost1} is the solution of the original problem and vice-versa. Initially, at time step $t=0$, the regressor vectors that form the matrix $\Phi(1|0)$ can be constructed from the constraints that form the initial model set $\mathcal{F}(0)$.

The vector $z\in {\mathbb R}^m$ is defined as:
\begin{equation*}
z=\sqrt{1-a^2}\left[\begin{array}{c} 1\\-a\\(-a)^2\\ \hdots \\ (-a)^{m-1} \end{array}  \right]\in {\mathbb R}^m,
\end{equation*}
and $Z$ is given as in \eqref{Eq:Z}.

In order to define $k \in {\mathbb R}^{n_u}$ and $n \in {\mathbb R}$, we first shortly recall the definition of the matrix determinant. For a square matrix $A\in {\mathbb R}^{N\times N}$, the determinant is defined as:
\begin{equation} \det (A)=\sum \limits_{\sigma \in S_N}\mbox {\rm sgn }(\sigma) \prod \limits_{i=1}^N A_{i,\sigma_i}, 
\end{equation} 
where $S_N$ denotes the set of all the permutations of the integers $\{1,\hdots,N\}$, $\mbox {\rm sgn } (\sigma)$ returns $1$ if the permutation $\sigma$ is even and $-1$ if the permutation is odd and $A_{i,\sigma_i}$ denotes the element of the matrix $A$ in the $i^{\text{th}}$ row and the column defined by the $i^{\text{th}}$ element in the permutation $\sigma$.\\
We rewrite the matrix $\Phi(t+1|t)$ as $\Phi(t+1|t)=\left[\Phi',\, W\varphi(t)+Zu(t|t)\right]$, where
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'=\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}  \Phi_{1,1} &\hdots & \Phi_{1,n_um-1}\\ \vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\ \Phi_{n_um,1}& \hdots & \Phi_{n_um,n_um-1} \end{array} \right],
\end{equation*}
with the elements $\Phi_{p,q}\in {\mathbb R},\,\, p=1,\hdots,n_um,\, q=1,\hdots,n_um-1$, denoting the corresponding elements of the applied regerssor vectors $\varphi(k),\, k=t-n_um+1,\hdots, t-1$. In addition, we denote by $S_{n_um}^i,\, i=1,\hdots, n_um$ the set of all the permutations of the integers $\{1,\hdots,n_um\}$ that have $n_um$ at the $i^{\text{th}}$ position. Based on this we define the vector $V \in {\mathbb R}^{n_um}$ as:
\begin{equation} V=\left[ \begin{array}{c}  \sum \limits_{\sigma \in S_{n_um}^1} sgn(\sigma) \prod\limits_{i=2}^{n_um}\Phi_{i,\sigma_i}\\ \sum \limits_{\sigma \in S_{n_um}^2} sgn(\sigma) \prod\limits_{i=1,\,i\neq 2}^{n_um}\Phi_{i,\sigma_i}\\ \vdots \\\sum \limits_{\sigma \in S_{n_um}^{n_um}} sgn(\sigma) \prod\limits_{ i=1,\, i\neq n_um}^{n_um}\Phi_{i,\sigma_i}\end{array} \right].
\end{equation}
Then we can express $k$ and $n$ by using $V$ as:
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
k^T&=V^TZ\\
n&=V^TW\varphi(t).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

We first note that, from the definition of the set $\mathcal{F}(t)$, it follows that constraints \eqref{Eq:output} are satisfied if and only if the following set of inequalities is satisfied:
\begin{equation} \begin{array}{ll}
\gamma_l(k)\leq p_l\!-\!\overline{d}_l, & \begin{array}{c} \forall l=1,\hdots,n_o\\\forall k\in [t+1,t+N]\end{array}\end{array}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation} \gamma_l(k)=\max \limits_{A_j(t)H_j\leq b_j(t)}\sum\limits_{j=1}^{n_y}e_{lj}\varphi(k|t)^TH_j.
\end{equation}

For fixed values of the vectors $\varphi(k|t), \, k\in [t+1,t+N]$, by using the fact that the inequalities $A_j(t) H_j\leq b_j(t),\, j=1,\hdots, n_y$ form nonempty, closed and bounded convex sets (i.e. polytopes), we can write the dual of the LP \eqref{Eq:Linear_proggrams_1} as:
\begin{eqnarray}
&\tilde{\gamma}_l(k)=\min \limits_{\lambda_l(k|t)}b(t)^T\lambda_l(k|t)\\
&\text{subject to} \nonumber\\ &A(t)^T\lambda_l(k|t)=\left[ \begin{array}{c}
e_{l1}\varphi(k|t)\\
\vdots\\
e_{ln_y}\varphi(k|t)\end{array}\right]\\ &\lambda_l(k|t)\geq \bold 0 .
\end{eqnarray}

Therefore, the optimization problem \eqref{Eq:FHOCP2_cost} can be solved by first solving the following two LPs:
\begin{eqnarray} J_1=\max \limits_{u(k|t)} k^T u(t|t)\\ J_2=\min \limits_{u(k|t)} k^T u(t|t) \end{eqnarray}
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{l}
\text{subject to}\\
\eqref{Eq:input_rate}, \, \eqref{Eq:end_constraint},\\
\left.\begin{array}{l}\!\!
A(t)^T\lambda_l(k|t)=\left[\begin{array}{c}
e_{l1}''\varphi(k|t)\\
\vdots\\
e_{ln_y}''\varphi(k|t)
\end{array}\right]\\
b(t)^T\lambda_l(k|t)\leq p_l''(k|t)\!-\!\sum\limits_{j=1}^{n_y}e_{lj}''\epsilon_{d_j}\\
\lambda_l(k|t)\geq \bold 0
\end{array}\!\!\! \right\}\begin{array}{cc}\forall l=1,\!\hdots,\!n_o\!+\!2n_y\\
\forall k\!\in\![t\!+\!1,t\!+\!N]\end{array}
\end{array}
\end{equation}
where $e_{lj}''$ stand for the element of the $l^\text{th}$ row and $j^\text{th}$ column of the matrix $E''$ and $p_l''(k|t)$ is the $l^\text{th}$ element of the vector $p''(k|t)$.

We rewrite the matrix $\Phi(t+1|t)$ as $\Phi(t+1|t)=\left[\Phi',\, W\varphi(t)+Zu(t|t)\right]$, where
\begin{equation*}
\Phi'=\left[ \begin{array}{ccc}  \Phi_{1,1} &\hdots & \Phi_{1,n_um-1}\\ \vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\ \Phi_{n_um,1}& \hdots & \Phi_{n_um,n_um-1} \end{array} \right],
\end{equation*}
with the elements $\Phi_{p,q}\in {\mathbb R},\,\, p=1,\hdots,n_um,\, q=1,\hdots,n_um-1$, denoting the corresponding elements of the applied regerssor vectors $\varphi(k),\, k=t-n_um+1,\hdots, t-1$.

In addition, we denote by $S_{n_um}^i,\, i=1,\hdots, n_um$ the set of all the permutations of the integers $\{1,\hdots,n_um\}$ that have $n_um$ at the $i^{\text{th}}$ position. Based on this we define the vector $V \in {\mathbb R}^{n_um}$ as:
\begin{equation} V=\left[ \begin{array}{c}  \sum \limits_{\sigma \in S_{n_um}^1} sgn(\sigma) \prod\limits_{i=2}^{n_um}\Phi_{i,\sigma_i}\\ \sum \limits_{\sigma \in S_{n_um}^2} sgn(\sigma) \prod\limits_{i=1,\,i\neq 2}^{n_um}\Phi_{i,\sigma_i}\\ \vdots \\\sum \limits_{\sigma \in S_{n_um}^{n_um}} sgn(\sigma) \prod\limits_{ i=1,\, i\neq n_um}^{n_um}\Phi_{i,\sigma_i}\end{array} \right].
\end{equation}

Then we can express $k$ and $n$ by using $V$ as:
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
k^T&=V^TZ\\
n&=V^TW\varphi(t).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
